WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.580
HTTPS protects transport confidentiality and integrity between the browser and the service endpoint it reaches.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:16.600
In ASP.NET Core applications, teams often combine HTTPS redirection and HSTS, but those controls solve different parts of the transport story.

3
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.180
Redirection helps move an HTTP request to HTTPS.

4
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:28.160
HSTS tells supporting browsers to use HTTPS for future requests to a host for a configured period.

5
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:35.480
HTTPS redirection is useful when a request arrives over HTTP and the application or hosting layer can redirect it to HTTPS.

6
00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.100
This should be reviewed with the real deployment path in mind.

7
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:50.720
Applications behind reverse proxies or load balancers need correct forwarded-header behavior so the app understands the original scheme and does not create confusing redirect behavior.

8
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.520
HSTS, or HTTP Strict Transport Security, is stronger in a different way.

9
00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:03.340
Once a supporting browser has received the policy, it can remember to use HTTPS for that host for the policy duration.

10
00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:09.040
ASP.NET Core provides HSTS middleware that is commonly enabled outside development.

11
00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.620
The setting should be treated as a deployment decision, not only a code setting.

12
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.660
MaxAge controls how long the browser should remember the policy.

13
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.360
IncludeSubDomains can extend the policy to subdomains.

14
00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:25.460
ExcludedHosts can keep specific hosts out of the middleware behavior.

15
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:33.160
Preload is a separate ecosystem-level decision where a domain may be submitted for browser preload lists when strict requirements are met.

16
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:36.380
Each choice has operational consequences.

17
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:42.100
IncludeSubDomains and preload require careful domain ownership and deployment review.

18
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:55.320
If subdomains, legacy hosts, partner-hosted names, or staging systems are not ready for HTTPS-only behavior, broad HSTS scope can create long-lived availability problems for users.

19
00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.980
Long durations are difficult to unwind quickly because browsers remember the policy.

20
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:08.660
The safe path is to test HSTS in staging and production-like environments before using long durations or broad subdomain scope.

21
00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:17.800
Confirm domain ownership, certificate coverage, proxy behavior, health checks, redirects, error pages, and operational rollback planning.

22
00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:24.860
HSTS is valuable, but it deserves the same change-management care as other browser-enforced policies.
